  "names": {
    "order": "given family",
    "conventions": "Most Black South Africans carry a name in their home language that means something (Sipho, gift; Lerato, love; Tumelo, faith; Ntsako, joy) and often an English or Christian name too (Lucky, Precious, Johannes), with a family name from the father's line. Zulu, Xhosa and Ndebele families also have a clan name, and calling someone by it (a Dlamini as Dlamini, a Mkhize as Khabazela) is warm and respectful. Sotho, Tswana and Pedi family names often start with Mo- or Ma- (Mokoena, Molefe, Maponya). Afrikaans family names come from Dutch, French Huguenot and German settlers (Van der Merwe, Du Plessis, Botha). Coloured families in the Cape carry English and Afrikaans names (Adams, Hendricks, September), Cape Muslim families spell Arabic names the Cape way (Mogamat, Gadija, Achmat), and Indian South Africans in Durban mostly carry Tamil, Telugu, Hindi and Gujarati family names (Naidoo, Pillay, Govender, Maharaj, Patel).",
